## Overview

QuillAudits is a Web3 security company that audits smart contracts. I worked there full time and remotely as a Graphic Designer, making graphics for QuillAudits and its sister brand QuillAI across marketing, the website, sales and events.

When I joined, the brand didn't have a cohesive visual identity. Colours were inconsistent, a lot of the visuals relied on generic stock illustration, and there were no clear guidelines. Much of my time went into building a consistent visual language that could carry across every touchpoint, while keeping up with day-to-day requests.

![Illustrative icons assembling in a short loop](/work/quillaudits/illustrative-icons-motion.webp "Illustrative icons, animated")

## My role

I was a graphic designer on the design team. My work covered:

- refining the colour palette, type and brand elements
- designing an icon system in two styles, standard and illustrative
- website assets, banners and cards
- social posts, blog thumbnails and announcements
- event creatives for side events and AMAs (ask-me-anything sessions)

The process was iterative and feedback-driven: research, ideation, concept, then implementation, with rounds of review in between.

## Visual identity

{% callout %}
The brief: enhance the existing brand while staying true to its core identity.
{% /callout %}

This is where the brand stood before. I kept the blue but pushed it to a more vibrant shade so it would stand out on digital platforms. I paired Chakra Petch for headings with Bai Jamjuree for body text. The logo's 45° angles became a wireframe grid, which I used as the base for icons, patterns and other brand assets.

![A selection of QuillAudits graphics from before the refresh](/work/quillaudits/previous-look.webp "The previous look")

{% gallery columns=2 %}
![Colour palette and typefaces](/work/quillaudits/colour-and-type.webp "Colours and type")

![The 45° brand grid, a pattern tile and the logo construction](/work/quillaudits/brand-grid-and-patterns.webp "Brand grid and patterns")
{% /gallery %}

With those pieces in place, the same angular shapes and blue gradients run through banners and social posts.

![Website banner reading 'Making Web3 a safer place'](/work/quillaudits/hero-banner.webp)

![A grid of social posts, blog thumbnails and announcements](/work/quillaudits/social-posts.webp "Social posts and thumbnails")

## Icon system

The existing icons mixed filled and outlined styles. I built a library of more than 100 icons in two styles. Standard icons sit on the brand grid with fixed stroke weights, so they stay clear at small sizes. Illustrative icons are 3D, made in Blender, and carry more detail for use at larger sizes.

